content='**Short answer**  \nThe failure is not caused by the `set_fact` or the `debug` call itself – it is caused by a *dynamic host pattern* that Ansible is trying to resolve and that simply does not exist in the inventory.  \nIn the snippet you posted the only place where a host pattern is built from a Jinja expression is in the `hostname:` line of the block:\n\n```yaml\n- block:\n    - set_fact:\n        hostname: "{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}"\n      when: inventory_hostname != \'localhost\'\n      ...\n```\n\nWhen the play is run against `localhost` the `when` clause is **false**, so the `set_fact` is skipped.  \nBecause the task is skipped, `register: set_hostname` is never created.  \nThe following `debug` task then tries to print `set_hostname`. Since that variable does not exist, Ansible falls back to looking for a host named `{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}` (the *un‑resolved* Jinja expression that would have been the value of `hostname`). The connection plugin then reports:\n\n```\ncouldn\'t resolve host \'{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}\'\n```\n\nSo the root cause is that the playbook (or the role) is using a dynamic host pattern that is not present in the inventory. In the new environment the inventory contains only `localhost`, so `{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}` cannot be resolved.\n\n---\n\n## Why this happens\n\n1. **Dynamic host patterns** – Any time you use a Jinja expression in a `hosts:` field, or in a `delegate_to:` field, Ansible will try to resolve the resulting string as a host name.  \n2. **Skipped tasks** – If a task is skipped (because of a `when:` condition), its `register:` value is *not* created.  \n3. **Unresolved variables** – When a variable is used in a host pattern but is not defined, the connection plugin reports *“couldn\'t resolve host …”*.\n\n---\n\n## How to fix it\n\n### 1. Stop using a dynamic host pattern that isn’t in the inventory\n\nIf you really need to run against a host whose IP is stored in `ansible_host`, make sure that host is defined in the inventory. For a simple `localhost` run you don’t need that dynamic pattern at all.\n\n**Option A – Use a static host list**\n\n```yaml\n- hosts: localhost\n  roles:\n    - my_role\n```\n\n**Option B – Add the host to the inventory**\n\n```ini\n[myhosts]\nmyserver ansible_host=192.168.1.42 ansible_connection=ssh\n```\n\n### 2. Remove or adjust the `when:` clause\n\nIf the `set_fact` is only meant to run on non‑localhost machines, move the `when:` clause outside the block and use a normal `set_fact`:\n\n```yaml\n- name: Set hostname if we are not on localhost\n  set_fact:\n    hostname: "{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}"\n  when: inventory_hostname != \'localhost\'\n```\n\n### 3. Guard the `debug` task\n\nIf you keep the block, guard the debug so it only runs when the variable is actually defined:\n\n```yaml\n- name: Debug the hostname\n  debug:\n    var: set_hostname\n  when: set_hostname is defined\n```\n\n---\n\n## Checklist for the new environment\n\n| Item | What to check | How |\n|------|---------------|-----|\n| **Inventory** | Does it contain a host called `localhost`? | `ansible-inventory --list` |\n| **Host variables** | Is `ansible_host` defined for that host? | Add to inventory or set in the playbook: `- hosts: localhost ansible_host=127.0.0.1` |\n| **Connection type** | For localhost you usually want `ansible_connection=local` | `- hosts: localhost ansible_connection=local` |\n| **Role tasks** | No stray Jinja expressions in a `hosts:` or `delegate_to:` field | Search the role for `{{ hostvars[...] }}` |\n| **Task skipping** | Make sure you understand when a task is skipped | Look at the `when:` clause and use `when: set_hostname is defined` before debugging |\n\n---\n\n## Quick diagnostic\n\nRun the playbook with increased verbosity to see exactly where the dynamic host pattern is being evaluated:\n\n```bash\nansible-playbook play.yml -vvv\n```\n\nYou should see something like:\n\n```\n<localhost> ESTABLISH SSH CONNECTION FOR USER: root\n...\n<localhost> EXEC ssh -C -tt -o ControlMaster=auto -o ControlPersist=60s ...\n```\n\nIf you see a line that looks like:\n\n```\n<localhost> ESTABLISH SSH CONNECTION FOR USER: root\n<localhost> EXEC ssh ... host=... \n```\n\nand the host name is the unresolved Jinja expression, that is the line that needs to be corrected.\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR\n\n* The error **“couldn\'t resolve host ‘{{ hostvars[inventory_hostname][\'ansible_host\'] }}’”** is a classic symptom of a dynamic host pattern that is not defined in the inventory.  \n* In the new environment you only have `localhost` in the inventory, so that dynamic expression cannot be resolved.  \n* Either run the play against a static host list (`localhost`) or add the host to the inventory.  \n* Guard the `debug` task with `when: set_hostname is defined` or remove the block entirely if it is not needed.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'gpt-oss:20b', 'created_at': '2025-10-17T13:53:52.265192223Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 591898548972, 'load_duration': 166592213, 'prompt_eval_count': 3678, 'prompt_eval_duration': 2421598771, 'eval_count': 50952, 'eval_duration': 589308461088, 'model_name': 'gpt-oss:20b'} id='run--5fed858b-765b-4f6b-8980-44f781a3dbd6-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3678, 'output_tokens': 50952, 'total_tokens': 54630}
