content='**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every input line we have to\n\n* take all words that contain **no digit**,\n* treat words that differ only in case as the same word,\n* output the distinct words in **alphabetical order**,\n* separate two consecutive words by one blank.\nIf a line contains no valid word an empty line has to be printed.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Observations\n\n* A word is a maximal sequence of English letters  \n  (`A–Z` or `a–z`).  \n  All other characters (digits, punctuation, spaces, …) are only separators.\n* Because we ignore case, every word can be stored in lower case.\n* The number of words on a single line is usually small –  \n  a simple set is sufficient to keep them unique.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Algorithm\nFor each input line\n\n```\ncreate an empty set S\nfor every maximal sequence of letters in the line\n        convert the sequence to lower case\n        insert it into S\noutput all elements of S in sorted order separated by one space\n```\n\nIf `S` is empty we simply output a newline.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm prints exactly the required words for each\ninput line.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nDuring the scan of a line the algorithm extracts exactly all maximal\nsequences of letters of the line.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nThe regular expression `[A-Za-z]+` matches a maximal contiguous block of\nletters.  \nEvery such block is a word according to the statement and the\ndefinition used by the problem.  \nNo other character is allowed inside a match, therefore no digit can\nappear in a matched word. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nEvery word that the algorithm inserts into the set `S`\ncontains no digit.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the algorithm only inserts strings that consist solely\nof letters.  \nHence no digit can be present in any inserted string. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nIf a word occurs more than once in a line, it is inserted into `S`\nonly once.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n`S` is a set, i.e. it contains each element at most once.\nAll inserted strings are converted to lower case, therefore two words\ndiffering only in case become the same string and can be stored only\nonce. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nThe set `S` after finishing the scan of a line contains\nexactly the distinct words of that line (ignoring case and digits).\n\n**Proof.**\n\n*Soundness.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 every element of `S` is a word without digits.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 each distinct word is inserted at most once.\nHence every element of `S` is a required word.\n\n*Completeness.*  \nLet `w` be any word of the line that contains no digit.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the regular expression matches `w` (because it consists\nonly of letters), the algorithm converts it to lower case and inserts\nit into `S`.  \nThus `w` (in lower case) is present in `S`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 5  \nThe algorithm outputs the elements of `S` in ascending lexicographical\norder and separates them by a single blank.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nThe algorithm copies all elements of `S` into a vector, sorts it with\n`std::sort` (which uses lexicographical order), and then prints the\nvector elements separated by blanks.  \nNo trailing or leading blanks are printed. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nFor every input line the algorithm prints exactly the list of distinct\nwords of that line that contain no digit, in alphabetical order,\nseparated by a single blank.  \nIf a line contains no such word, it prints an empty line.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 the set `S` contains precisely the required words.\nBy Lemma&nbsp;5 the output consists of the elements of `S` in the\nrequired order and format.  \nIf `S` is empty the loop that prints the words does not output any\ncharacter, only a newline – exactly an empty line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Complexity Analysis\n\nLet `n` be the length of a line and `k` the number of words\n(extracted from that line).\n\n*Scanning the line* – `O(n)`  \n*Inserting into the set* – `O(k log k)`  \n*Sorting the final list* – `O(k log k)`  \n\nThus the total running time per line is `O(n + k log k)`  \nand the memory consumption is `O(k)`.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (C++17)\n\n```cpp\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    string line;\n    /* matches maximal blocks of letters (no digits) */\n    const regex word_regex("[A-Za-z]+");\n\n    while (getline(cin, line)) {\n        unordered_set<string> uniq;                 // distinct words, lower case\n\n        for (auto it = sregex_iterator(line.begin(), line.end(), word_regex);\n             it != sregex_iterator(); ++it) {\n            string w = it->str();                  // the word\n            transform(w.begin(), w.end(), w.begin(), ::tolower); // case‑insensitive\n            uniq.insert(w);\n        }\n\n        /* output in ascending order */\n        vector<string> words(uniq.begin(), uniq.end());\n        sort(words.begin(), words.end());\n\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < words.size(); ++i) {\n            if (i) cout << \' \';\n            cout << words[i];\n        }\n        cout << \'\\n\';\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the GNU++17 compiler.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'gpt-oss:20b', 'created_at': '2025-10-17T15:28:32.774188688Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 355619673088, 'load_duration': 163363365, 'prompt_eval_count': 1555, 'prompt_eval_duration': 121088584, 'eval_count': 31094, 'eval_duration': 355333659559, 'model_name': 'gpt-oss:20b'} id='run--73ac4522-b360-451c-921c-5654841234d9-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1555, 'output_tokens': 31094, 'total_tokens': 32649}
